meta {
  name: List links - authenticated by cookie
  type: http
  seq: 15
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/links
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: snip_access={{accessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("authenticates on the cookie alone, with no Authorization header", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.links).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("scopes to the same account the header would have", function () {
    const codes = res.body.links.map((link) => link.code);

    expect(codes).to.not.include("riv4l99");
  });

  test("still sets no cookie of its own — reading one grants no power to write one", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("set-cookie")).to.be.undefined;
  });
}

docs {
  research.md D10a. The browser sends the access token as an httpOnly cookie the
  Next.js BFF set on a domain both origins share; a mobile app or a CLI sends
  the same token in `Authorization: Bearer`. This request is the first of those
  two, done by hand.

  It is one credential read from two places, not two kinds of credential. The
  signature check, the claims, and the expiry are identical either way, and the
  header wins if a client somehow sends both — a header had to be constructed on
  purpose, while a cookie rides along and cannot express intent.

  Why a cookie at all: the token used to live in a JavaScript variable, which
  died with the page, so every reload spent a refresh token to mint another.
  That is not a security property — it is a consequence of where the value was
  kept, and it made two tabs opening at once look to the server like theft. An
  httpOnly cookie cannot be read by script at all, and a refresh now happens
  only when the token has actually expired.

  Both cookies are `Path=/`, and which one means "signed in" is the part worth
  knowing:

  - `snip_refresh` is the session. The dashboard's route guard tests for it, and
    for nothing else.
  - `snip_access` is a transient. It expires every fifteen minutes and its
    absence is an ordinary, recoverable state — deleting it by hand should cost
    one refresh request, not a sign-out. A revision of the guard that tested
    this cookie instead did sign people out, which is why the distinction is
    written down in three places.

  Neither puts a credential on the redirect path in production: the short domain
  is a different registrable domain from the one `SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN` names,
  so no session cookie is sent there at all. Locally they share `localhost` and
  the access cookie does arrive — where it is read by nothing, since the
  redirect runs in middleware ahead of the router. Principle V governs what is
  *set* on that path, and this API cannot set a cookie anywhere.

  Rails still has no cookie middleware. `request.cookies` is Rack parsing a
  request header, which grants no ability to set one — hence the third test.

  Variations worth pasting in by hand:

      Cookie: snip_access=nonsense        → 401 unauthenticated
      Cookie: snip_access=                → 401 unauthenticated
      (an access token older than 15 min) → 401 token_expired
}
